Q.2 Which one is the fastest among the four techniques that you discussed? 
1) WHERE & IN
SELECT DISTINCT user_id
FROM Table_A
WHERE user_id IN (
    SELECT user_id
    FROM Table_B
);
2) JOIN & IS NULL: Slowest
SELECT DISTINCT a.user_id
FROM Table_A a
INNER JOIN Table_B b
    ON a.user_id = b.user_id;
3) INTERSECT 
SELECT user_id
FROM Table_A
INTERSECT
SELECT user_id
FROM Table_B;
4) EXISTS
SELECT DISTINCT a.user_id
FROM Table_A a
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
    FROM Table_B b
    WHERE b.user_id = a.user_id
);
5) USING MAX: Fastest
SELECT user_id
FROM (
    SELECT user_id, 'A' AS source FROM Table_A
    UNION ALL
    SELECT user_id, 'B' AS source FROM Table_B
) t
GROUP BY user_id
HAVING MAX(CASE WHEN source = 'A' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) = 1
   AND MAX(CASE WHEN source = 'B' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) = 1;

| Method         | Efficiency                      | Notes                              |
| -------------- | ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| **INNER JOIN** | ✅ Best for most RDBMS           | Fast with proper indexing          |
| **INTERSECT**  | ✅ Efficient but limited support | Cleaner syntax, removes duplicates |
| **EXISTS**     | ✅ Efficient with large `TableA` | Good alternative to joins          |
| **IN**         | ❌ Slower on large `TableB`      | Avoid if possible                  |
| **MAX**        | ✅ Most Efficient       


